# User rules (stand workstation)

These rules apply in every project I open.

## Everywhere

- Answer in the language I write in.
- Run the project's tests before you say a change is done, and show the last line of the run.
- Ask before you add a dependency.
- Never push to a shared branch.
- Keep a change to one concern; put unrelated fixes in a list at the end of your answer.
- Do not reformat lines the task does not touch.
- When a command fails, show the error before you try something else.
- Prefer small functions with names that say what they return.
- Write commit messages in the imperative mood, under 72 characters in the first line.
- If a task is ambiguous, ask one question before you start, not five after.
- Do not leave commented-out code behind.
- Keep secrets out of code, logs and commit messages.

## Frontend (web/**, *.tsx, *.ts, *.css)

- Use function components and hooks; no class components.
- Keep component files under 200 lines; split by responsibility, not by size alone.
- Co-locate a component's test next to it as `<Name>.test.tsx`.
- Use the design tokens in `web/theme/` for colours and spacing; never hard-code a hex value.
- Every interactive element needs an accessible name.
- Use `<button>` for actions and `<a>` for navigation.
- Do not add a state library; React state and context are enough here.
- Fetch data through the hooks in `web/api/`, never with a bare `fetch` in a component.
- Type every prop; no `any`, and no `as` casts without a comment saying why.
- Keep CSS modules per component; no global selectors outside `web/theme/global.css`.
- Lazy-load routes that are not on the first screen.
- Format dates with the helpers in `web/lib/dates.ts`, never with `toLocaleString` directly.
- Strings a user reads go through the translation helper `t()`.
- Images get explicit width and height to avoid layout shift.
- Run `npm run lint` and `npm test -- --run` before you say a frontend change is done.
- Snapshot tests are not allowed; assert on what the user sees.
- Do not disable an ESLint rule inline; fix the code or ask me.
- Use `data-testid` only when no role or label can find the element.
- Keep effects free of data transformation; derive values during render.
- Debounce search inputs by 300 ms.

## Django apps (backend/**, *.py under backend/)

- One app per bounded context; do not import models across apps, use the service layer.
- Every model change ships with its migration in the same commit.
- Never edit a migration that has been released; add a new one.
- Use `select_related` and `prefetch_related` where a view lists related objects.
- Put business logic in `services.py`, not in views or serializers.
- Views return serializers' data; no hand-built dicts.
- Use the settings module `backend.settings.local` for local runs only.
- Every endpoint needs a permission class; `AllowAny` needs a comment saying why.
- Time zones: store UTC, convert at the edge.
- Use `transaction.atomic` around writes that touch more than one table.
- Tests use factories from `backend/tests/factories.py`, not fixtures in JSON.
- Mark slow tests with `@pytest.mark.slow`.
- Celery tasks must be idempotent; pass ids, not model instances.
- Log with the module logger, never `print`.
- Validate input in serializers, not in models' `save`.
- Admin classes live in `admin.py` and register every model.
- Management commands take `--dry-run` when they write.
- Do not raise bare `Exception`; use the errors in `backend/core/errors.py`.
- Keep URL names in snake case.
- Pin new packages in `requirements/base.in` and recompile the lock.

## Go services (services/**, *.go)

- Wrap every returned error with context: `fmt.Errorf("load user %d: %w", id, err)`.
- Pass `context.Context` as the first argument to anything that does I/O.
- No package-level mutable state.
- Table-driven tests for anything with more than two cases.
- Use `errors.Is` and `errors.As`, never string matching on errors.
- Keep interfaces small and defined where they are used.
- Close every `io.Closer` with `defer` right after the error check.
- Run `go vet ./...` and `staticcheck ./...` before you say a Go change is done.
- Log with `slog`; key names in snake case.
- Timeouts on every outbound HTTP client.
- Goroutines need a way to stop; no fire-and-forget.
- Prefer `sync.WaitGroup` or `errgroup` to channels for fan-out with errors.
- Config comes from environment variables read once at start-up.
- Do not use `init()` for anything but registering drivers.
- Keep `main.go` under 50 lines; wiring only.
- Generated files carry the `// Code generated ... DO NOT EDIT.` header.
- Benchmarks for anything on the request path that allocates in a loop.
- Use `time.Duration` for durations in APIs, never ints.
- Return early; avoid `else` after a `return`.
- Vendor nothing; use modules.

## Terraform (infra/**, *.tf)

- Run `terraform fmt` and `terraform validate` before you say an infra change is done.
- Never run `terraform apply`; I apply.
- Every resource gets the `owner` and `env` tags.
- Pin provider versions with `~>` to a minor version.
- Remote state only; never commit a `.tfstate` file.
- Use modules from `infra/modules/`; do not copy resources between environments.
- Variables have a description and a type.
- Outputs that hold secrets are marked `sensitive = true`.
- Security groups list ports explicitly; no `0.0.0.0/0` on anything but the load balancer.
- Use `for_each` over `count` for anything with a stable key.
- Name resources in snake case.
- Keep one environment per directory under `infra/envs/`.
- Data sources over hard-coded ids.
- IAM policies are documents in `infra/policies/`, not inline JSON.
- Plan output goes in the pull request description.
- Lifecycle `prevent_destroy` on databases and buckets.
- No provisioners.
- Default to the smallest instance size that passes the load test.
- Document every manual step in `infra/RUNBOOK.md`.
- Destroying anything needs my yes in the chat first.

## SQL migrations (migrations/**, *.sql)

- One change per migration file.
- Every migration has a down migration that has been run once locally.
- Add columns as nullable, backfill, then add the constraint in a later migration.
- Create indexes concurrently on tables over a million rows.
- Never rename a column in one step; add, copy, switch, drop.
- No data deletes in a schema migration.
- Name files `YYYYMMDDHHMM_<what>.sql`.
- Foreign keys get an index.
- Use `timestamptz`, never `timestamp`.
- Check the plan of any query you add with `EXPLAIN`.
- Keep enum changes additive.
- Lock timeouts at the top of each migration.
- Test migrations against a copy of production size, not an empty database.
- Default values on large tables are added without a rewrite.
- Do not use `SELECT *` in views.
- Primary keys are `bigint` identities.
- Comments on tables and columns that are not obvious.
- Partition only after measuring.
- No triggers without my yes.
- Grants go in their own migration.

## iOS (ios/**, *.swift)

- SwiftUI for new screens; UIKit only where SwiftUI has no equivalent.
- No force unwraps outside tests.
- View models are `@MainActor`.
- Use `async`/`await`; no new completion handlers.
- Strings go through `Localizable.strings`.
- Images come from the asset catalogue.
- Keep views under 150 lines; extract subviews.
- Dependency injection through initialisers.
- Run the unit tests on the latest two iOS versions before you say an iOS change is done.
- No third-party UI libraries.
- Use `Logger` with a subsystem and category.
- Accessibility labels on every image button.
- Dynamic Type must not truncate primary content.
- Network calls go through `APIClient`.
- Keychain for tokens, never `UserDefaults`.
- Feature flags read from `FeatureFlags.swift`.
- Previews for every view.
- No storyboards.
- Keep `Info.plist` keys sorted.
- Crash-free rate is checked before every release.

## Android (android/**, *.kt)

- Jetpack Compose for new screens.
- Coroutines with structured concurrency; no `GlobalScope`.
- ViewModels expose `StateFlow`, never mutable state.
- Hilt for dependency injection.
- Strings in `strings.xml`.
- No `!!` outside tests.
- Room for local storage; migrations tested.
- Run `./gradlew lint test` before you say an Android change is done.
- Use `WorkManager` for deferrable background work.
- Network through the Retrofit service in `data/api/`.
- Content descriptions on every icon button.
- Keep composables pure; side effects in `LaunchedEffect`.
- Baseline profiles updated before a release.
- No reflection-based serialization; use kotlinx.serialization.
- ProGuard rules next to the module that needs them.
- Feature modules do not depend on each other.
- Use `Timber` for logging.
- Encrypted shared preferences for tokens.
- Test on the minimum SDK as well as the latest.
- Screenshots in the pull request for any UI change.

## Data notebooks (notebooks/**, *.ipynb)

- Clear outputs before you commit a notebook.
- Read data through `notebooks/lib/load.py`, never from a hard-coded path.
- Pin the random seed at the top.
- No credentials in a notebook, not even for a minute.
- Move code used twice into `notebooks/lib/`.
- Plots get titles and labelled axes.
- Keep one question per notebook.
- Record the data snapshot date in the first cell.
- Use `pathlib` for paths.
- Large intermediate files go in `data/cache/`, which git ignores.

## Docs (docs/**, *.md)

- One sentence per line in Markdown sources, so diffs stay small.
- Headings in sentence case.
- Every page starts with one paragraph saying who it is for.
- Code blocks carry a language tag.
- Link to the source file rather than copying code into a page.
- Screenshots get alt text and a date in the file name.
- Use relative links inside the docs folder.
- Keep the changelog in `CHANGELOG.md`, newest first.
- Spell out an abbreviation the first time a page uses it.
- Run the link checker before you say a docs change is done.
- Do not document internal endpoints in public docs.
- Put a "last checked" date on any page that describes a third-party tool.
- Keep tutorials runnable from a fresh clone.
- Prefer a table to a long list of "if X then Y" sentences.
- One page per task; split a page that answers two questions.
